WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Metacognition matters in day-to-day work because real tasks are full of assumptions.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.020
In debugging and troubleshooting,

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.400
it helps you ask what you have actually verified and what you are only assuming.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.140
In code review,

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.020
it helps you separate surface familiarity from real understanding of the data flow or

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:14.480
risk.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.360
In writing and analysis, it helps you check whether the evidence supports the claim.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.120
Teams benefit when reasoning is visible.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.120
Shared assumptions are easier to improve when people say them out loud.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.785
A useful team habit is asking, what are we missing?

00:00:25.785 --> 00:00:30.070
Another is naming uncertainty clearly: this is confirmed, this is likely, this is unknown,

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.285
and this is the next test.

00:00:31.285 --> 00:00:35.150
These habits improve communication because they make confidence and evidence easier to

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:35.960
inspect.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.500
AI-assisted work makes metacognition even more important.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.750
AI tools can produce fluent output, but fluency is not the same as correctness.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.620
A user still needs to check assumptions, sources, constraints, and uncertainty.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.370
Tool fluency can create the feeling of understanding because the answer arrives quickly

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.840
and sounds polished.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.080
Verified understanding requires review, application, and evidence.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.395
A practical AI workflow includes metacognitive checkpoints.

00:00:58.395 --> 00:00:59.760
What did I ask the tool to do?

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.480
What assumptions did the output make?

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.820
Which parts can I verify?

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:03.820
What evidence is missing?

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.880
Do I understand the result well enough to defend or modify it?

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.505
The goal is not to reject tools.

00:01:08.505 --> 00:01:11.915
The goal is to use them critically while keeping responsibility for judgment and

00:01:11.915 --> 00:01:12.795
understanding.

00:01:12.795 --> 00:01:15.120
This is also useful for delegation and review.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.070
When you hand work to another person or a tool,

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:21.160
keep track of what standard the output must meet and how you will verify it.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.975
Metacognition keeps the owner of the task engaged.

00:01:22.975 --> 00:01:26.460
It prevents a smooth handoff from becoming a loss of accountability, context,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.440
or understanding.
